        },
        "valueString": "No costovertebral angle tenderness. No obvious external hernia. Testes non-tender with no swelling."
      }
    },
    {
      "fullUrl": "https://example.org/fhir/Observation/observation-16",
      "resource": {
        "resourceType": "Observation",
        "id": "observation-16",
        "status": "final",
        "code": {
          "text": "Toenail findings"
        },
        "subject": {
          "reference": "Patient/patient-1"
        },
        "valueString": "Bilateral great toenails yellow-brown discoloured and thickened, with distal involvement approximately 40–50% on the right and 30–40% on the left. Some subungual debris and mild onycholysis. Other toenails mildly affected, less than 20%. No current pain, surrounding redness, swelling, or discharge. No surrounding erythema or warmth on examination; no obvious tinea pedis today. Patient reports the thick yellow toenails have been present for a couple of years, slowly increasing, mainly affecting the great toes; nails are crumbly and cosmetically bothersome. Intermittently tried over-the-counter athlete’s foot cream; no nail lacquer. Denies history of psoriasis, dermatitis, or nail trauma."
      }
    },
